  "response": "The judge's resolution of a credibility conflict of this kind is no academic exercise, because it fixes the evidentiary posture in which the case is tried. Either the confession was admitted into evidence at the defendant's trial or it was excluded from evidence at that trial: one of those two outcomes obtained, and the two cannot both have obtained.\n\nCourts assess voluntariness under a totality of the circumstances test. The weight given to the defendant's age, education, and intelligence shows that this is the operative standard, since characteristics of that personal kind could matter only to an inquiry directed at the whole of the circumstances rather than at some single formal defect. Because the inquiry takes in the circumstances as a whole, it drives courts to test the competing accounts against objective corroboration such as booking logs, medical records, and time-stamped custody sheets. The corroboration assembled in such a record indicates that the defendant's account of what occurred during the interrogation was inaccurate in part. At least one of the two narratives was inaccurate in part\u2014the defendant's or the interrogating officers'\u2014and perhaps both were.\n\nThe prosecution bears the burden of proving that a contested confession was voluntary, and the measure of that burden is fixed in the first instance by federal constitutional law. Lego v. Twomey established that the federal constitutional minimum for proving voluntariness is a preponderance of the evidence. Lego v. Twomey required the prosecution to prove voluntariness beyond a reasonable doubt in every court in the United States; a nationwide command of proof beyond a reasonable doubt and a constitutional floor fixed at a mere preponderance cannot both stand. Whereas the federal minimum is satisfied by a preponderance, some states require the prosecution to prove voluntariness beyond a reasonable doubt as a matter of state law.\n\nBecause Jackson v. Denno requires a trial judge to make a reliable determination of voluntariness outside the presence of the jury, the judge is led to resolve irreconcilable testimony by assessing the demeanor and consistency of the competing witnesses. Jackson v. Denno held that the jury alone decides in the first instance whether a confession was voluntary; that allocation of the question to the jury and any requirement that the judge reliably settle voluntariness outside the jury's hearing cannot both be true, since the one commits the issue to the very body that the other excludes from it.",
